course: history
lesson: 65
title: Light, machines and medicine: how the last 250 years changed a normal day
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Toby's friend says Fleming found penicillin and saved everybody in like a day. Abby tells her gran it is «delulu», short for deluded, to say the washing machine changed more lives than the internet. One claim is a myth, the other is a real debate.
explain: What really happened: Fleming noticed in 1928 that a mould killed germs, but it took Florey, Chain and many others until the 1940s to turn it into a medicine. And some economists really do argue that home machines like washers freed more time than the internet, while others disagree.
explain: Picture a power cut: candles, a cold fridge, no TV. Around 1800 that was every evening. Light bulbs, fridges, cars, phones, vaccines, soap and painkillers for operations nearly all came in the last 250 years.
explain: The biggest change is the saddest to remember: around 1800 about four in ten children died before age five, and today about four in a hundred do. Clean water, vaccines, better food and medicine made that difference.
explain: Every change had people behind it and new problems after it. Workers campaigned for weekends, doctors who said «wash your hands» were laughed at first, and plastic, once a wonder, now litters beaches.
fact: Electric light bulbs became practical around 1880; in 1930 only about one US farm in ten had electricity, and about 700 million people still live without it today.
fact: Washing clothes by hand once took a whole day each week, mostly for women; whether home machines changed life more than the internet is a real debate.
fact: Electric fridges became common in American homes by about 1950 and in many other countries later; before, people shopped often and kept food cool in cellars or with ice.
fact: In 1796 Edward Jenner used cowpox to protect people from smallpox; earlier, people in China, India, Africa and the Ottoman Empire used a riskier method, and an enslaved African man, Onesimus, told Boston doctors about it in 1721.
fact: Smallpox was wiped out worldwide in 1980.
fact: In 1847 Ignaz Semmelweis showed handwashing saved mothers' lives but was mocked; in the 1860s to 1880s Pasteur and Koch proved that germs cause disease.
fact: Fleming noticed in 1928 that mould killed bacteria; Florey, Chain and others turned penicillin into a medicine in the 1940s; antibiotics do not work against viruses like colds.
fact: Until 1846, when ether was shown working in Boston, most surgery was done on patients who were awake.
fact: Around 1800 about 4 in 10 children died before age five; today about 4 in 100 do.
fact: Karl Benz built a motor car in 1886, the Wright brothers flew in 1903, and 66 years later people walked on the Moon.
fact: Bell patented the telephone in 1876, though others such as Antonio Meucci worked on similar ideas and credit is argued; radio spread in the 1920s and TV from the 1950s.
fact: Early factory workers often worked 12 hours or more, six days a week; workers campaigned, and the 8-hour day and two-day weekend spread in the early 1900s.
fact: Plastics spread from the 1950s; they are cheap, light and clean, but plastic waste now fills oceans and beaches.
exercise: When did electric light bulbs become practical? | Around 1880 | Around 1500 | Around 1980 | Around 1066
exercise: About how many people still live without electricity today? | About 700 million | Nobody at all | About 7 | Everyone in Europe
exercise: Who mostly did the long weekly washing by hand? | Women | Kings and queens | Soldiers in barracks | Washing robots
exercise: How did people keep food cool before fridges? | In cellars or with ice | In ovens | In the sunshine | They could not keep food at all
exercise: What did Edward Jenner use against smallpox in 1796? | Cowpox | Penicillin pills | Crushed garlic | Boiled sea water
exercise: Who told Boston doctors about protecting against smallpox in 1721? | Onesimus, an enslaved African man | Louis Pasteur, the French chemist | Alexander Fleming, a Scottish doctor | Henry Ford, a car maker
exercise: When was smallpox wiped out worldwide? | In 1980 | In 1796, by Jenner | In about 1066 | It still spreads today
exercise: What did Semmelweis show in 1847? | Handwashing saved mothers' lives | Germs are harmless to most people | Fridges stop illness in hospitals | Bad smells cause all disease
exercise: Who proved that germs cause disease? | Pasteur and Koch | Benz and Ford | Bell and Meucci | Jenner and Onesimus
exercise: Who turned penicillin into a medicine in the 1940s? | Florey, Chain and others | Fleming alone in one day | Edward Jenner | The Wright brothers
exercise: Which illness do antibiotics not cure? | A cold, caused by a virus | An ear infection by bacteria | A bacterial chest infection | An infected cut
exercise: What changed surgery in 1846? | Ether was shown to stop pain | The first cloth bandage was made | Electric light in hospitals | The telephone for calling doctors
exercise: Around 1800, how many children died before age five? | About four in ten | About four in a hundred | None at all | Nearly all of them
exercise: When did the Wright brothers fly a plane? | In 1903 | In about 1803 | In 1969, in July | In about 1886
exercise: Why is the credit for the telephone argued about? | Others like Antonio Meucci worked on similar ideas | Nobody ever made a working telephone at all | It was really invented by the ancient Romans | Bell was a made-up person in a newspaper story
exercise: How did the weekend come about? | Workers campaigned for shorter hours | It was always there in nature | Factories invented it for fun | Kings banned work on Mondays
exercise: What problem did plastic bring? | Waste that fills oceans and beaches | It made medicines dirty and unsafe | It was too heavy to carry far | It stopped all trade between countries
test: toby's mate says fleming saved everyone in like a day lol. what's the real story | Fleming spotted it; a team took years to make the medicine | Correct, one man in one day, then everyone was cured | Nah, penicillin was only found in 2020 | Fleming invented the vaccine for cows, not penicillin, duh
test: Abby says Gran is delulu about the washing machine. What is fair? | It is a real debate; home machines freed huge amounts of time | Gran is totally wrong: washing machines changed nothing at home | Washing machines came before fire, so they're nothing special | Gran's right, but only because the internet came in 1800
test: «shut that fridge, u'll waste the cold» says Mum. What did families do before fridges? | Shopped often and kept food in cellars or with ice | Ate only frozen pizza from the shop every day | Never ate any fresh food at all, only bread | Kept their food in the oven, where it always stayed cool
test: The nurse says vaccines are a long story that starts with cows. Who else belongs in it? | People in China, India, Africa and Onesimus in Boston | Only Jenner; nobody else had any idea before him | The Wright brothers, who flew vaccines around the world | Nobody: cows invented it and people just copied
test: In 1847 Semmelweis told doctors to wash their hands and they laughed. What would happen without his idea winning? | Far more people would die from germs | Nothing, germs do not exist | Doctors would get cleaner anyway by magic | Hospitals would close for ever
test: Fatima asks: before anaesthetics, were people awake for operations? | Yes, until ether was shown in 1846 | No, surgery was always painless | No, there was no surgery at all | Yes, but only in 2000
test: Leo hears that in 1800 four in ten kids died young. Why do so few die today? | Clean water, vaccines, better food and medicine | People just got luckier than they used to be | Children stopped going outside to play | Doctors found one magic potion that cures everything
test: Jasper's grandad's grandad saw the first car in the village. How quickly did travel change? | From the first planes to the Moon in 66 years | It took 2,000 years | Cars came after the Moon landing | It never changed at all
test: Ava laughs at a dial phone; Grandad says it was cutting edge, kid. When did TV spread? | Mostly from the 1950s | Back in the early 1700s | Only in the 2020s | In Roman times, lol
test: Mason moans about school at 9; his dad says factory kids in 1830 started at 6. Why do we have weekends? | Workers fought for shorter hours | Kings gave them away for fun | Nature made Saturdays | Computers chose them
test: Your game gives a tech that is pure upside. Which real invention shows help and harm together? | Plastic: useful and clean, but it litters seas | Soap: no downside at all, so it's the perfect pick | Glasses: they helped some but made everyone else blind | Fridges: handy at first, but they made all food rot
test: ugh a cold, gimme antibiotics. What would the doctor say? | Antibiotics do not work on viruses like colds | Yes, antibiotics cure every illness, colds too | Only if you take ten of them at once with juice | Antibiotics are only for cows, not for people
exam: Identify the persons who developed penicillin into a usable medicine during the nineteen-forties. | Howard Florey, Ernst Chain and their colleagues | Alexander Fleming alone, within a single day | Edward Jenner and Onesimus | Karl Benz and Henry Ford
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning child mortality. | It fell from about four in ten children in 1800 to about four in one hundred today | It has remained constant since 1800, at about four children in ten | It has risen tenfold since 1800, owing chiefly to the growth of cities | It was eliminated entirely in the year 1900 throughout the world
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the origins of vaccination? | Earlier practices in several lands preceded Jenner's use of cowpox in 1796 | Vaccination was devised by a single person without any precedent whatsoever | Vaccination was devised in the twentieth century by Louis Pasteur | Vaccination is ineffective against smallpox and was abandoned
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for the adoption of the two-day weekend in the early twentieth century. | Sustained campaigning by workers for reduced hours | A natural law of the calendar observed since antiquity | A decree of the Roman Empire revived by later kings | The invention of plastic and of household machines
